{"links":{"self":"/works/OL2865967W/editions.json?limit=100","work":"/works/OL2865967W"},"size":8,"entries":[{"publishers":["Penguin Books, Limited","Allen Lane"],"subtitle":"A New History","source_records":["bwb:9780713999280","amazon:0713999284"],"title":"Cold War","number_of_pages":400,"isbn_13":["9780713999280"],"languages":[{"key":"/languages/eng"}],"full_title":"Cold War A New History","lc_classifications":[""],"publish_date":"2006","key":"/books/OL28982653M","authors":[{"key":"/authors/OL432543A"}],"works":[{"key":"/works/OL2865967W"}],"type":{"key":"/type/edition"},"subjects":["Cold war","World politics, 1945-"],"covers":[13976958],"latest_revision":2,"revision":2,"created":{"type":"/type/datetime","value":"2020-08-20T04:24:28.390690"},"last_modified":{"type":"/type/datetime","value":"2023-04-14T13:20:58.682246"}},{"publishers":["HighBridge Company"],"subtitle":"A New History","description":{"type":"/type/text","value":"It began during the Second World War, when American and Soviet troops converged from east and west. Their meeting point—a small German city—became part of a front line that solidified shortly thereafter into an Iron Curtain. It ended in a climactic square-off between Ronald Reagan’s America and Gorbachev’s Soviet Union. In between were decades of global confrontation, uncertainty, and fear.\r\n\r\nDrawing on new and often startling information from newly opened Soviet, Eastern European, and Chinese archives, this thrilling account explores the strategic dynamics that drove the Cold War, provides illuminating portraits of its major personalities, and offers much fresh insight into its most crucial events. Riveting, revelatory, and wise, it tells a story whose lessons it is vitally necessary to understand as America once more faces an implacable ideological enemy.\r\nsource: https://highbridgeaudio.com/coldwar.html"},"contributors":[{"role":"Narrator","name":"Jay Gregory and Alan Sklar"}],"covers":[9293793],"physical_format":"Digital Audio","last_modified":{"type":"/type/datetime","value":"2020-03-09T08:55:55.467826"},"latest_revision":3,"key":"/books/OL27925357M","isbn_13":["9781598873757"],"classifications":{},"title":"The Cold War","identifiers":{"goodreads":["52227682"]},"created":{"type":"/type/datetime","value":"2020-03-09T08:53:20.767595"},"languages":[{"key":"/languages/eng"}],"isbn_10":["159887375X"],"publish_date":"2005-12-12","oclc_numbers":["966136529"],"works":[{"key":"/works/OL2865967W"}],"type":{"key":"/type/edition"},"revision":3},{"publishers":["Penguin Books","Penguin"],"identifiers":{"amazon":["0141025328"]},"covers":[8791368],"physical_format":"paperback","full_title":"The Cold War","lc_classifications":["D843"],"key":"/books/OL27325454M","authors":[{"key":"/authors/OL432543A"}],"source_records":["amazon:0141025328","bwb:9780141025322","promise:bwb_daily_pallets_2023-05-23:KS-364-616","idb:9780141025322"],"title":"The Cold War","number_of_pages":352,"isbn_13":["9780141025322"],"isbn_10":["0141025328"],"publish_date":"Jan 25, 2007","works":[{"key":"/works/OL2865967W"}],"type":{"key":"/type/edition"},"local_id":["urn:bwbsku:KS-364-616"],"latest_revision":6,"revision":6,"created":{"type":"/type/datetime","value":"2019-09-29T21:18:12.439549"},"last_modified":{"type":"/type/datetime","value":"2023-12-19T20:57:13.280805"}},{"publishers":["ALLEN LANE (PENG)"],"identifiers":{"librarything":["161718"],"goodreads":["336420"]},"weight":"1.6 pounds","covers":[452551],"physical_format":"Hardcover","key":"/books/OL7798474M","authors":[{"key":"/authors/OL432543A"}],"source_records":["ia:coldwarnewhistor00gadd_292","bwb:9780713999129"],"title":"Cold War","number_of_pages":352,"isbn_13":["9780713999129"],"isbn_10":["0713999128"],"publish_date":"January 5, 2006","works":[{"key":"/works/OL2865967W"}],"type":{"key":"/type/edition"},"physical_dimensions":"9.1 x 6.1 x 1.3 inches","lc_classifications":["D843"],"latest_revision":12,"revision":12,"created":{"type":"/type/datetime","value":"2008-04-29T15:03:11.581851"},"last_modified":{"type":"/type/datetime","value":"2025-03-28T15:40:35.124821"}},{"identifiers":{"goodreads":["28432"],"librarything":["161718"]},"title":"The Cold War","subtitle":"A New History","authors":[{"key":"/authors/OL432543A"}],"publish_date":"December 26, 2006","publishers":["Penguin"],"table_of_contents":[{"level":0,"label":"Preface","title":"ix","pagenum":"","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":0,"label":"List of Maps","title":"xv","pagenum":"","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":0,"label":"Prologue: The View Forward","title":"1","pagenum":"","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":0,"label":"I The Return of Fear","title":"5","pagenum":"","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":0,"label":"II Deathboats and Lifeboats","title":"48","pagenum":"","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":0,"label":"III Command Versus Spontaneity","title":"83","pagenum":"","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":0,"label":"IV The Emergence of Autonomy","title":"156","pagenum":"","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":0,"label":"V The Recovery of Equity","title":"156","pagenum":"","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":0,"label":"VI Actors","title":"195","pagenum":"","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":0,"label":"VII The Triumph of Hope","title":"237","pagenum":"","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":0,"label":"Epilogue: The View Back","title":"259","pagenum":"","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":0,"label":"Notes","title":"267","pagenum":"","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":0,"label":"Bibliography","title":"299","pagenum":"","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":0,"label":"Index","title":"317","pagenum":"","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}}],"covers":[7318124,111423,-1],"physical_format":"Paperback","languages":[{"key":"/languages/por"}],"isbn_13":["9780143038276"],"isbn_10":["0143038273"],"type":{"key":"/type/edition"},"source_records":["amazon:0143038273"],"key":"/books/OL7361702M","number_of_pages":352,"works":[{"key":"/works/OL2865967W"}],"latest_revision":11,"revision":11,"created":{"type":"/type/datetime","value":"2008-04-29T13:35:46.876380"},"last_modified":{"type":"/type/datetime","value":"2026-01-03T22:34:15.272979"}},{"subtitle":"A New History","covers":[9293787],"local_id":["urn:phillips:31867001379408","urn:sfpl:31223049152730","urn:sfpl:31223049152714","urn:sfpl:31223049152706","urn:sfpl:31223049152771","urn:sfpl:31223049152623","urn:sfpl:31223049152631","urn:sfpl:31223049152607","urn:nuls:31786102090112"],"ia_loaded_id":["coldwarnewhistor00gadd"],"lc_classifications":["D843 .G22 2005","D843.G22 2005"],"ocaid":"coldwarnewhistor00gadd","description":"Many will remember what it was like to live under the shadow of the Cold War, the ever-present anxiety that at some point, because of some miscalculation or act of hubris, we might find ourselves in the middle of a nuclear holocaust—a war that , if we survived it, would change our lives and our planet forever.\r\n\r\nHow did this terrible conflict arise? How did wartime allies so quickly become deadly foes after 1945 and divide the world into opposing camps, each armed to the teeth? And how, suddenly, did it all come to an end? Only now that the Cold War has been over for fifteen years can we begin to find a convincing perspective on it. John Lewis Gaddis’s masterly book is the first full, major history of the whole conflict and explains not just what happened, but why it happened—why the Soviet Union brutally repressed rebellion in East Germany, Hungary, and Czechoslovakia; how Kennedy and Khrushchev confronted each other over the Cuban Missile Crisis; why Nixon and Mao Zedong sought wary friendship; what, at the end, John Paul II, Ronald Reagan, Margaret Thatcher, and Mikhail Gorbachev each thought they were doing. Gaddis has synthesised all the most recent scholarship, but has also used minutes from Politburo meetings, startling information from recently opened Soviet and Asian archives, conversations between leaders overheard and noted down by their aides, and above all, the words of the leading participants themselves—showing what was really on the mind of each, with a very dramatic immediacy.\r\n\r\nWith the judgement of a master history, Gaddis shows what the underlying dynamics of the conflict were—how politics and ideology interact with each other, how changes in society were as important as changes in government, and how ideas of morality affected (or didn’t affect) what politicians actually did. Finally, in a work who’s interpretive authority equals its narrative power, he how’s how policy makers at the top—and ordinary people at the bottom—reversed the course of history thereby achieving one of the greatest victories ever for the human spirit.\r\n—jacket","subject_time":["1945-1989."],"edition_name":"printing (2)","source_records":["marc:marc_records_scriblio_net/part15.dat:149583510:641","marc:marc_loc_updates/v35.i20.records.utf8:21050520:895","marc:marc_ithaca_college/ic_marc.mrc:214313182:915","ia:coldwarnewhistor00gadd","marc:marc_openlibraries_phillipsacademy/PANO_FOR_IA_05072019.mrc:63913761:2126","marc:marc_openlibraries_sanfranciscopubliclibrary/sfpl_chq_2018_12_24_run03.mrc:137511754:3518","marc:marc_loc_2016/BooksAll.2016.part32.utf8:175912120:895","bwb:9781594200625","marc:marc_columbia/Columbia-extract-20221130-012.mrc:7534022:1576","marc:marc_nuls/NULS_PHC_180925.mrc:122449084:2321","idb:9781594200625","marc:harvard_bibliographic_metadata/ab.bib.09.20150123.full.mrc:795087325:1750"],"title":"The Cold War","languages":[{"key":"/languages/eng"}],"subjects":["Cold War","World politics -- 1945-1989"],"publish_country":"nyu","by_statement":"John Lewis Gaddis.","oclc_numbers":["895213643","61303540"],"type":{"key":"/type/edition"},"publishers":["Penguin Press"],"ia_box_id":["IA171501"],"physical_format":"Hardcover","key":"/books/OL3428737M","authors":[{"key":"/authors/OL432543A"}],"publish_places":["New York, USA"],"pagination":"xii, 333p.","classifications":{},"